  "summary": "Claire Lomas, a 44-year-old charity campaigner from Melton, Leicestershire, died on 22 August following an accident in Jordan. She became widely known after being paralysed from the chest down in a 2007 riding accident at the Osberton Horse Trials, which caused multiple severe injuries including a broken neck, back, and ribs, and a punctured lung. Despite her paralysis, she raised nearly £1 million for charity through endurance events, most notably completing the 2012 London Marathon in 17 days using a bionic suit. She also lit the Paralympic cauldron in Trafalgar Square later that year and participated in the 2016 Great North Run while pregnant—finishing the half marathon in five days after being denied entry to the Vitality London 10k. Her family expressed profound grief and requested privacy during their mourning."
}
```

